// implement-follows-an-approach checks that implement NAMES an approach. It does not check
// that the named one exists.
//
//     phase "implement" state="complete" followed="an approach nobody recorded"
//
// passes. So does a design-system that produced three approaches while implement followed
// one — the other two are decisions the record says were taken, and were not.
//
// AZ2 asked whether the plan was written before the code, which is unverifiable and was also
// the wrong question. The code IS the decision; the plan is the educated act before it, and
// what it buys is that the alternatives were in view when the decision was taken. What that
// requires is not chronology. It is that the plan and the thing built AGREE.

thin-slice "TS.260821.20" {
    slug "the-plan-and-the-code-agree"
    title "Refuse an approach the record says was taken and was not"
    kind "command"
    realizes "CAP.claim-settlement"
    from-storm "ES.260819.01"

    command "check"
    actor "whoever closes an iteration that planned something"
    produces-event "ApproachHonoured" "ApproachAbandoned"
    needs-read-model "what-this-iteration-claims-and-has-shown"

    trigger "an iteration records a plan and builds something the plan does not describe"
    outcome "every approach the record holds was either followed or is recorded as abandoned, and a `followed` naming nothing dangles"

    scenario #"""
        Given an iteration whose design-system produced approaches
        When implement is complete
        Then each approach was followed, or says why it was not — because an approach recorded
             and not taken is a decision the record CLAIMS was made, which is worse than no plan
             at all: no plan is silence, and this is an account of a choice nobody made.
        """#

    step "1" does="`followed` names an approach the same iteration produced. One naming nothing dangles, the way every other unresolved edge does"
    step "2" does="every approach produced is followed, or carries `abandoned` saying what happened instead"
    step "3" does="abandoning is normal and cheap. Building teaches things a plan cannot know, and a rule that made abandonment expensive would teach people to record only what they had already built"
    step "4" does="an abandoned approach stays in the record. Deleting it loses the alternative that was considered, which is the whole reason the plan exists"

    excludes "checking that the CODE matches the approach. No record can read an implementation \
        and say whether it is the thing that was described — that is what the claims and their \
        evidence are for, one layer down, and pretending otherwise would be the strongest \
        overclaim in this frame"
    excludes "requiring an approach per claim, or per file, or any finer granularity. The unit is \
        the decision, and a decision that shaped four claims is one decision"

    layer "doctrine"    reaches="abandoning a plan is a recorded act, and the skill says it is expected rather than a failure"
    layer "enforcement" reaches="a `followed` naming no approach is refused; an approach neither followed nor abandoned is refused"
    layer "docs"        reaches="`what did they plan, and what did they actually do` is answerable per iteration"

    claim "C1" text="a `followed` naming an approach the iteration did not produce is refused" \
        settled-by="a test naming an approach nothing recorded, asserting a refusal — the case that passes today"
    claim "C2" text="an approach neither followed nor abandoned is refused" \
        settled-by="a test with two approaches and one `followed`, asserting the unfollowed one is named — a decision the record claims was taken and was not"
    claim "C3" text="an abandoned approach is recorded, not deleted, and says what happened instead" \
        settled-by="a test asserting an `abandoned` with no reason is refused, and that an abandoned approach still satisfies the rule while staying in the record"

    depends-on "TS.260821.19 — there are approaches to agree with"

    useful-alone "a plan stops being able to describe work that was never done. Today an iteration \
        can record three approaches, build against none of them, name a fourth that does not \
        exist, and close clean"

    attacks "S2"

    useful-to "the-team-building-a-digital-product"

    cut-from "ITER.260822.14/AZ2, superseded — the maintainer: the code is the decision, and the \
        plan is the informed act before it"

    principle-note "an artifact looks identical whether the agent reasoned hard or pattern-matched \
        a template. A plan makes the reasoning legible, and an unfollowed plan makes it legible \
        and false — which is worse, because the reader now has an account to trust"

    proves "the plan the record holds is the plan that was built against"
    does-not-prove "that the code is any good, or that the approach was right. It refuses a plan \
        that describes work nobody did; whether the work was worth doing is what the claims settle"
}
